'''''Vicia pannonica''''' is a species of [[Vicia|vetch]] known by the common name '''Hungarian vetch'''.<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17}}</ref> It is native to southern, central Europe and western Asia, and it is sometimes cultivated as an [[Agriculture|agricultural crop]] for use as [[hay]] and [[fodder]].<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20111002132725/http://ecocrop.fao.org/ecocrop/srv/en/cropView?id=10810 FAO EcoCrops]</ref> It may [[escaped plant|escape cultivation]] and grow as a casual roadside [[weed]].

==Description==
This is an annual herb producing a hairy, climbing stem supported by the [[tendril]]s on its leaf-tips. The leaves are each made up of 10 to 20 oval or oblong leaflets measuring up to 2.5 centimeters in length. The [[inflorescence]] bears two to four pealike flowers each up to 2 centimeters long. The flower corolla is yellow or purple-marked and the back of the banner (the large top petal) is coated in soft hairs. The fruit is a hairy [[legume]] pod 2 to 3 centimeters long.

